**Proof.**

Place the incircle as the unit circle centered at the incenter $I=(0,0)$. For a point of tangency at angle $\theta$ on the unit circle, the corresponding side is the tangent line
$$x\cos\theta + y\sin\theta = 1.$$

Let $D=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)$, $E=(\cos\beta,\sin\beta)$, $F=(\cos\gamma,\sin\gamma)$. Since the configuration is invariant under rotation of the whole picture about $I$, we may set $\alpha=0$, so that $D=(1,0)$ and
$$BC:\ x=1.$$

**Vertices $B$ and $C$.** The vertex $B=AB\cap BC$ lies on $x=1$ and on the tangent at $F$:
$$\cos\gamma + y\sin\gamma = 1 \implies y=\frac{1-\cos\gamma}{\sin\gamma}=\tan\frac{\gamma}{2}.$$
So $B=\bigl(1,\tan\frac{\gamma}{2}\bigr)$. Similarly $C=AC\cap BC$ gives
$$C=\Bigl(1,\tan\frac{\beta}{2}\Bigr).$$
Set $p=\tan\frac{\beta}{2}$, $q=\tan\frac{\gamma}{2}$. Then $B=(1,q)$, $C=(1,p)$, and the midpoint of $BC$ is
$$M=\Bigl(1,\frac{p+q}{2}\Bigr).$$

**The line $GH$.** Since $G$ and $H$ are the reflections of $E$ and $F$ through $I$, we have $G=-E$ and $H=-F$. The chord $EF$ of the unit circle has equation
$$x\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}+y\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=\cos\frac{\beta-\gamma}{2}.$$
Reflecting through the origin ($x\mapsto -x,\ y\mapsto -y$) sends line $EF$ to the parallel line $GH$:
$$x\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}+y\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=-\cos\frac{\beta-\gamma}{2}.\tag{$\ast$}$$

**The point $Q=BC\cap GH$.** Substituting $x=1$ into $(\ast)$:
$$\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}+y\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=-\cos\frac{\beta-\gamma}{2},$$
so
$$y_Q=\frac{-\cos\frac{\beta-\gamma}{2}-\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}}{\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}}.$$
Using $\cos\frac{\beta-\gamma}{2}+\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=2\cos\frac{\beta}{2}\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}$ and $\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=\sin\frac{\beta}{2}\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}+\cos\frac{\beta}{2}\sin\frac{\gamma}{2}$, and writing these in terms of $p=\tan\frac{\beta}{2}$, $q=\tan\frac{\gamma}{2}$:
$$\cos\frac{\beta}{2}\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{(1+p^2)(1+q^2)}},\qquad \sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=\frac{p+q}{\sqrt{(1+p^2)(1+q^2)}}.$$
Therefore
$$y_Q=-\frac{2\cos\frac{\beta}{2}\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}}{\sin\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}}=-\frac{2}{p+q},$$
and
$$Q=\Bigl(1,-\frac{2}{p+q}\Bigr).$$

**Perpendicularity.** Since $I$ is the origin, the vectors $\overrightarrow{IQ}$ and $\overrightarrow{IM}$ are simply $Q$ and $M$. Their dot product is
$$Q\cdot M = (1)\!\left(1\right)+\left(-\frac{2}{p+q}\right)\!\left(\frac{p+q}{2}\right)=1-1=0.$$

Hence $\overrightarrow{IQ}\cdot\overrightarrow{IM}=0$, which means the lines $(IQ)$ and $(IM)$ are perpendicular. $\blacksquare$

